# Speaker notes of the Chapter 4 deck v3_8_0, slides 1-40. Reader-facing full sentences, 60-140 words, no presenter directions, no everyday analogies.
NOTES_A = {
1: "Chapter 4 follows one secret number through three forms: a private key, a public key and an address. The three ovals on the title slide show the order: the number becomes a point on a curve, and the point becomes a short string that can be read aloud. Chapter 3 showed the software that checks the rules of the network; this chapter opens the smallest secret that those rules protect. Every result in this deck was computed by the chapter library under Python 3.14.6, and no Bitcoin Core node was run for the slides.",
2: "The chapter has five parts around one key, and the tiles on the right count the slides, the true stories and the code examples. The part ranges are jump targets: the questions and the chapter map come first, then public key cryptography, legacy addresses, script hashes with Bech32, private key formats and advanced keys. The first part after the map looks like pure mathematics, but every later address format depends on it, so it is worth reading before the address parts. Each of the three true stories is told in plain sentences, and each of the twenty-two code examples is followed by a picture of its result.",
3: "Six questions structure the chapter, and each has its answer on a slide. They run from the private key through the public key, the legacy address, the newer address formats and the private key formats to the case of a valid address with no key behind it. The fourth question, about P2SH, Bech32 and Bech32m, is the one that usually needs the most reading, because three formats are involved. The sixth question is not a trick: it is answered by the last true story, and the summary slide returns to all six in one table.",
4: "This picture is the skeleton of the lecture: entropy, private key, public key, hash and address. Each arrow runs forward easily and backwards never, which means that every stage can be computed from the one before it while the earlier stage cannot be recovered from the later one. If one arrow could be reversed, a thief could climb back from a published address to the secret key, so the whole design rests on these one-way steps. A frequent mistake is to believe that the address contains the public key; it contains only a hash of it.",
5: "Part 2 begins with three ideas: the private key, the public key and the curve secp256k1. A private key is a secret 256-bit number, and whoever knows it can spend the coins. A public key is a point on the curve that is computed from the private key and is safe to publish. The curve is the one that Bitcoin uses: y squared equals x cubed plus 7. The table at the bottom shows where the divider sits among the five parts of the chapter, so the reader always knows which part is open.",
6: "This table collects the first six concepts of the chapter with a one-line meaning for each. Keys and addresses are the secret numbers that control coins and the public names that receive them. Public key cryptography uses two linked keys, one secret and one shareable. A private key is a secret 256-bit number, a public key is a point on the curve, a key pair is the two together, and entropy counts how unpredictable a secret is in bits. The concepts return on later slides with code that computes each of them.",
7: "The second concept table covers the mathematics behind the public key. Elliptic curve cryptography builds keys from points on a curve and a multiplication that cannot be undone. The curve secp256k1 is the one Bitcoin uses, and the generator point G is the fixed point from which every public key is counted. The point at infinity is the zero of the curve, because adding it to a point changes nothing. Elliptic curve multiplication adds a point to itself k times, and it is done quickly by repeated doubling, as the code slides will show.",
8: "In June 1976 Whitfield Diffie and Martin Hellman, cryptographers at Stanford University, sent the paper New Directions in Cryptography to a journal; the journal stamped it received on 3 June 1976. Until then secret keys had to travel to every partner. They proposed a key in two halves: each user publishes one half, keeps the other, and a one-way computation links them. That is the machinery of this chapter, in which a point is multiplied, the result is published and the multiplier is kept. Both authors later received the ACM Turing Award for 2015 for this work.",
9: "A digital signature proves that the holder of a private key approved a message, without revealing the key. The signer mixes the hash of the message with the private key and a fresh number, and two numbers, r and s, come out. The message, the signature and the public key travel together, and none of them is secret. Anyone can recompute the check from the public key, and only a signature made with the matching private key passes. Changing one letter of the message changes the hash in 135 of 256 bits, so the old signature fails; the table shows three such checks.",
10: "A lambda is a function without a name, written on one line at the place where it is needed. The four tiles name its parts: the keyword lambda, the input x, the colon that ends the inputs, and the value that it hands back. Here the value is the test 0 < x < K.N, which is true for a valid private key and false otherwise. The table runs five inputs through it, and the def card shows the same helper with a name. Both forms give the same answers, and the name ok only labels the function. Three later code slides use a lambda.",
11: "A private key is any whole number from 1 to n minus 1, where n is the order of the curve. The code takes the private key of the book's example and tests five numbers against the range: zero and n fail, and one, the book's key and n minus 1 pass. The result table repeats the verdicts with the size of each number in bits. The book's key has 253 bits, and the largest valid key has 256. There are about 2 to the power 256 valid keys, and the range is the only rule: randomness is a separate duty that the next slides cover.",
12: "Entropy counts how many bits of guessing an attacker faces, and it equals the length of the secret times the base-2 logarithm of the size of the alphabet. The code computes it for four secrets: a four-digit PIN has 13.3 bits, a lowercase word of eight letters 37.6 bits, a password of twelve symbols 78.7 bits, and 256 fair coin flips exactly 256 bits. The chart shows how far the first three fall below the last. Only truly random choices reach the 256 bits of a Bitcoin key, because every predictable choice removes bits from the count.",
13: "A key is only as safe as the source of its randomness. The secrets module draws from the secure random source of the operating system, so an observer learns nothing useful from the numbers it produces. The random module uses the Mersenne Twister, a fast generator with a fixed internal state: after 624 outputs of 32 bits in a row, that state can be recovered and every later number predicted. For this reason random is for simulation and never for keys. The call randbelow(m) gives 0 to m minus 1, and adding 1 yields the range 1 to n minus 1 of a private key.",
14: "The curve secp256k1 is defined by one short formula, y squared equals x cubed plus 7, counted modulo a large prime p. The code takes the generator point G, the fixed starting point that every key multiplies, and checks that both sides of the formula agree for it. The table confirms that G lies on the curve, that p has 256 bits, that p equals 2 to the 256 minus 2 to the 32 minus 977, and that the x coordinate of G begins with 0x79be667ef9. One formula and one fixed point are all that is needed to start.",
15: "A finite field of prime order p is the set of whole numbers from 0 to p minus 1 with addition, subtraction, multiplication and division carried out modulo p. The row of numbers 0 to 16 shows the wrap: after 16 the count starts again at 0. The table works four examples modulo 17: 13 plus 7 gives 3, 3 minus 5 gives 15, 5 times 7 gives 1, and 1 divided by 5 gives 7. Division by 5 is multiplication by 7. Bitcoin's prime has 78 digits, and every curve coordinate stays inside 256 bits with exact answers and no rounding.",
16: "The curve secp256k1 is fixed by a short list of numbers: the equation, the prime p of 78 digits, the order n of about 1.1579 times 10 to the 77, and the two coordinates of the generator G. Every public key is a multiple of the same G on this curve. A standard fixes the numbers so that every program computes on the same curve and gets the same key. SEC 2 lists the recommended parameters; secp256k1 is in SEC 2 but not on the NIST list, while secp256r1 is on both. Bitcoin chose a curve with a simple equation and a equal to 0.",
17: "One doubling turns a point on the curve into a new point in three steps. First the tangent to the curve at G is drawn. Second, the tangent meets the curve once more at a third point. Third, that point is reflected in the x-axis, and the mirror image is 2G. The blue and green arrows on the figure mark these steps, and the labels G, 2G, 4G and 8G show how repeated doubling reaches far points quickly. The figure is Figure 4-4 of Mastering Bitcoin, third edition, and it is used under its CC BY-SA 4.0 licence.",
18: "Adding two points follows one rule: a line through the points meets the curve in a third point, and the mirror image of that point is the sum. The picture shows the 17 points of a toy curve over 17 numbers, and the eight teal points are the multiples of P = (1, 5). The table lists four sums: P + P gives (2, 10) by the tangent rule, P + 2P gives (5, 9), 2P + 2P gives (12, 1), and P + (-P) gives the point at infinity, because a vertical line meets the curve nowhere else. Adding P to itself repeatedly yields every multiple kP, which is how a private key k becomes a public key.",
19: "Multiplying G by a number k is done by doubling and adding, so even a 256-bit k needs only a few hundred steps. The code multiplies G by one, two, three and four, and then adds G to itself and compares the sum with the second multiple: they are the same point. The table shows the first eight hexadecimal digits of the x coordinate for each multiple. For the private key of the book's example the multiplication needs 252 doublings and 123 additions, which is a very small amount of work for a very large number.",
20: "On a toy curve over only 17 numbers the multiples of a point can be listed in full. The code walks the multiples of P = (1, 5) and looks for the first one that is None, the point at infinity. The ninth multiple is None, so the walk closes into a cycle after nine steps and then repeats. The table lists the nine values from (1, 5) up to infinity. Real keys behave in the same way, but the cycle of secp256k1 has length n, so nobody can walk it.",
21: "Each extra bit of a key doubles the work of an attacker who searches for it. The code takes five key sizes in bits, 20, 40, 80, 128 and 256, and prints the average number of tries as a power of ten: 6, 12, 24, 38 and 77. On average half of all keys must be tried. A 20-bit key falls after about 524,288 tries, which a laptop does in an instant, while a 256-bit key needs about 10 to the 77 tries. The chart shows the exponents so that the huge numbers can be compared at a glance.",
22: "Going forward is quick, and going back is a search. Finding k from K = kG is the discrete logarithm problem, and with no known shortcut it means trying every k, a brute-force search. The row of boxes shows a toy case: 2 to the k equals 7 modulo 101 is found after nine tries. The table compares the toy group with secp256k1, where about 5.8e+76 tries are needed on average, which would take 1.8e+57 years at a trillion tries a second. The universe is only about 1.4 x 10 to the 10 years old.",
23: "The code of this chapter is written to be read, not to guard money. The sketch performs one point addition for every 1-bit of the key, so its running time tells an onlooker how many 1-bits the key has: the three bars show 0, 123 and 252 additions for three keys. A library such as libsecp256k1 does the same work in the same time for every key and has years of public review. The sketch was checked against Bitcoin Core 31.1 and the BIP 350 vectors, but it must never hold real funds; for real money use a tested cryptographic library.",
24: "Part 3 turns a public key into the legacy address that people read aloud. P2PKH, pay to public key hash, stores only a 20-byte hash of the key in the output script. HASH160 is the hash itself: SHA256 followed by RIPEMD-160, giving 20 bytes. Base58Check is the text form: a version byte, the payload and a checksum written in base58. The table at the bottom marks this part within the five parts of the chapter. The seventeen slides of this part first define the vocabulary and then compute each step with the chapter library.",
25: "The first concept table of this part covers scripts and the first Bitcoin addresses. An output script locks coins with a condition, and an input script supplies the data that unlocks them. The earliest payments went to an IP address, and the first output script, pay to public key (P2PK), held the full public key. A legacy address for P2PKH is a short text that names the hash of a public key and starts with 1. The table gives each concept a one-line meaning, and the following slides compute them one at a time.",
26: "The second concept table covers hashes. P2PKH holds only a 20-byte hash, which is a commitment: a hash that fixes a value now and lets the owner reveal it later. SHA256 turns any input into a 32-byte fingerprint, and RIPEMD-160 gives a 20-byte fingerprint. HASH160 is SHA256 followed by RIPEMD-160, the 20-byte hash of a public key. Base58Check encoding writes a version byte, a payload and a checksum in base58. These terms are used together on slide 36, where the whole address is built step by step.",
27: "The third concept table covers the text format of a legacy address. Base58 is an alphabet of 58 letters and digits without look-alike characters. The checksum is four bytes of a double SHA256 that catch typing mistakes. The version prefix is the first byte, which says what follows: 00 for a key hash and 05 for a script hash. A compressed public key has 33 bytes, the x coordinate and one byte that says whether y is even or odd, while an uncompressed key has 65 bytes, a 04 byte and then x and y in full.",
28: "The same number can be written in different bases, and a larger base uses fewer characters. The 33-byte public key needs 264 characters in binary, 66 in hexadecimal, 44 in base64 and 45 in base58. The table shows the symbols and the bits that each symbol carries: 1 for base 2, 4 for hexadecimal, 6 for base64 and 5.86 for base58. A bit is 0 or 1 and a byte is 8 bits, so a 65-byte public key has 520 bits. Base58 gives up a little density for an alphabet without the characters 0, O, I and l.",
29: "The earliest Bitcoin software let a spender type the receiver's IP address. The payer's node connected to the receiver's node, the receiver answered with a fresh public key, and the payer sent coins to that key. The method faded because the receiver had to be online and reachable, and nothing proved who had answered. An address replaced it: a short text that anyone can write down and pay at any time. The rest of this part shows how such an address is built from a public key, step by step.",
30: "A public key has two encodings. The code multiplies G by the private key of the book's example and writes the resulting point in both forms. The compressed form keeps x and one parity byte, 02 or 03, and has 33 bytes; the uncompressed form writes 04, x and y and has 65 bytes. The result pair (33, 65, 3, 4) shows the two lengths and the two prefix bytes. The compressed key is 49 percent shorter, and decompressing it returns the same point, which the last line confirms as True.",
31: "A P2PK output script holds the public key itself, while a P2PKH script holds only a 20-byte hash of it, a commitment to the key. The code builds both scripts for the compressed key of the book's example: the P2PK script has 35 bytes and the P2PKH script 25. The table lists what each one holds. The hash inside P2PKH equals HASH160 of the key, and both scripts end in opcode 0xac, which is CHECKSIG. The shorter script also keeps the key hidden until the owner spends the coins.",
32: "A script runs on a stack. Data is pushed on top of a list, and each opcode takes the top items and pushes its result. The slide runs a P2PKH script step by step: the unlocking data places the signature and the public key on the stack, OP_DUP copies the key, OP_HASH160 hashes the copy, the hash from the locking script is pushed, and OP_EQUALVERIFY removes both equal hashes. OP_CHECKSIG then checks the signature against the key and leaves true. The script passes when a nonzero item is on top of the stack at the end.",
33: "HASH160 is two hashes in a row. The code takes the 33-byte public key, computes SHA256 to get a 32-byte fingerprint, and then RIPEMD-160 to shorten it to 20 bytes. The result tuple (33, 32, 20, True) shows the three lengths and confirms that the two steps equal the library function hash160. The chart plots the number of bytes after each stage. The 20 bytes begin with bbc1e42a, and this is the value that sits inside every P2PKH script and legacy address of the book's example key.",
34: "A hash is a commitment to a key. The address holds only the 20-byte hash of the public key, which begins bbc1e42a. To spend, the owner shows the public key itself, 33 bytes, together with a signature, and anyone can hash the shown key and compare the result with the hash in the address. The table compares two keys: the real public key matches, and the same key with one bit flipped does not, because 78 of the 160 hash bits change. A short hash is easy to print and hides the key until the moment of spending.",
35: "On 8 January 2009 Satoshi Nakamoto, the pseudonymous author of Bitcoin's first software, announced version 0.1. The copyright line of the file base58.h names him, and right below it sits a design memo with four reasons. People copy addresses by hand, in e-mail and by double-click, so the text must survive all three. Of 62 letters and digits the four look-alikes 0, O, I and l are dropped, and so is all punctuation, so 58 symbols remain. The picture is a bust used as a stand-in, because the face of the author is unknown. The file is public on GitHub.",
36: "Base58Check turns a key hash into an address in three steps. The version byte 00 goes in front of the 20-byte hash; the checksum is the first four bytes of a double SHA256 of that body; and the 25 bytes are written in base58. The result tuple (25, 34, '1') shows that the address has 34 characters and starts with 1. The table splits the 25 bytes into version, key hash and checksum 37fefcd0. The address 1J7mdg5rbQyUHENYdx39WVWK7fsLpEoXZy matches the output of the one-step library function, which the last line confirms as True.",
37: "This figure shows the whole pipeline from public key to address, and each arrow is a function that the previous slides have already run. The public key has 33 bytes, SHA256 gives 32 bytes, RIPEMD-160 gives the 20-byte key hash, the version and checksum make 25 bytes, and base58 writes them as 34 characters. The sizes in the text boxes are the ones that were measured with the chapter library. The figure is Figure 4-7 of Mastering Bitcoin, third edition, used under its CC BY-SA 4.0 licence.",
38: "A P2PKH address is a short label for the 25-byte locking script of the output. The script holds the opcodes 76 (OP_DUP), a9 (OP_HASH160), the push 14 of 20 bytes, the key hash, 88 (OP_EQUALVERIFY) and ac (OP_CHECKSIG). The address of the same output holds a 00 version byte, the same 20-byte key hash and a 4-byte checksum, written as 34 characters. The table shows that the two forms of one public key give two different addresses: the compressed key gives 1J7mdg5rbQyUHENYdx39WVWK7fsLpEoXZy and the uncompressed key gives 1424C2F4bC9JidNjjTUZCbUxv6Sa1Mt62x.",
39: "The checksum catches almost every single typing mistake. The code starts from the example address and builds every one-character substitution: 1,938 variants in all. It then counts how many of them are still valid Base58Check strings, and the answer is 0. The table repeats the counts, and the original address is valid. A random typo passes the four-byte checksum about once in 4,294,967,296 tries. The check protects against mistakes in typing, but it does not protect against someone who writes a different valid address.",
40: "Mainnet and testnet look different on purpose, so that a test payment can never reach the main chain. The table compares the signs of the two networks: the P2PKH version byte is 00 on the main network, so the address starts with 1, and 6F on the test network, so it starts with m; the P2SH version byte is 05 against C4, giving the starts 3 and 2; and the Bech32 human-readable part is bc against tb. A wallet that reads a test address tb1q as a main address finds nothing and refuses it.",
}
